  "cut_point": "We are given a sequence:\u000A\u000A$$\u000Aa_n = 1 + n^3\u000A$$\u000A\u000Aand the sequence starts as:\u000A\u000A$$\u000A\\{2, 9, 28, 65, \\ldots\\}\u000A$$\u000A\u000AWe are also given:\u000A\u000A$$\u000A\\delta_n = \\gcd(a_{n+1}, a_n)\u000A$$\u000A\u000AWe are to find the **maximum** value that $\\delta_n$ can take.\u000A\u000A---\u000A\u000A### Step 1: Understand the sequence\u000A\u000AWe are told:\u000A\u000A$$\u000Aa_n = 1 + n^3\u000A$$\u000A\u000ASo:\u000A\u000A$$\u000Aa_1 = 1 + 1^3 = 2 \\\\\u000Aa_2 = 1 + 2^3 = 9 \\\\\u000Aa_3 = 1 + 3^3 = 28 \\\\\u000Aa_4 = 1 + 4^3 = 65 \\\\\u000A$$\u000A\u000ASo the first few terms match the given sequence.\u000A\u000AWe are to compute:\u000A\u000A$$\u000A\\delta_n = \\gcd(a_{n+1}, a_n)\u000A$$\u000A\u000Aand find the **maximum** value of $\\delta_n$.\u000A\u000A---\u000A\u000A### Step 2: Use the formula for $\\delta_n$\u000A\u000AWe are to compute:\u000A\u000A$$\u000A\\delta_n = \\gcd(a_{n+1}, a_n) = \\gcd(1 + (n+1)^3, 1 + n^3)\u000A$$\u000A\u000ALet’s simplify:\u000A\u000A$$\u000Aa_{n+1} = 1 + (n+1)^3 = 1 + n^3 + 3n^2 + 3n + 1 = a_n + 3n^2 + 3n\u000A$$",
